= #text("Weeds: An Appreciation / 为杂草说几句话")

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("A weed, says the dictionary, is \"any plant that is useless, troublesome, noxious or grows where it is not wanted.\" The dictionary also adds: \"colloq., a cigar.\" We may omit for our present purpose the harmless colloquialism, but the rest of the definition deserves to be closely examined. Socrates, I imagine, could have found a number of pointed questions to put to the dictionary maker. He might have begun with two of the commonest weeds, the nettle and the dandelion. Having got his opponent—and the opponents of Socrates were all of the same mental build as Sherlock Holmes's Dr Watson—eagerly to admit that the nettle was a weed, he would at once put the definition to the test. \"The story goes,\" he would say, quoting Mrs. Clark Nuttall's admirable work, Wild Flowers as They Grow, \"that the Roman soldiers brought the most venomous of the stinging nettles to England to flagellate themselves with when they were benumbed with the cold of this—to them—terribly inclement isle. It is certain,\" he would add from the same source, \"that physicians at one time employed nettles to sting paralysed limbs into vigour again, also to cure rheumatism. In view of all this,\" he would ask, \"does it not follow either that the nettle is not a weed or that your definition of a weed is mistaken?\" And his opponent would be certain to answer: \"It does follow, O Socrates.\" A second opponent, however, would rashly take up the argument. He would point out that even if the Romans had a mistaken notion that nettle-stings were useful as a preventive of cold feet, and if our superstitious ancestors made use of them to cure rheumatism, as our superstitious contemporaries resort to bee-stings for the same purpose, the nettle was at all times probably useless and is certainly useless to-day. Socrates would turn to him with a quiet smile and ask: \"When we say that a plant is useless, do we mean merely that we as a matter of fact make no use of it, or that it would be of no use even if we did make use of it?\" And the reply would leap out: \"Undoubtedly the latter, O Socrates.\" Socrates would then remember his Mrs. Nuttall again, and refer to an old herbal which claimed that \"excessive corpulency may be reduced\" by taking a few nettle-seeds daily. He would admit that he had never made a trial of this cure, as he had no desire to get rid of the corpulency with which the gods had seen fit to endow him. He would claim, however, that the usefulness of the nettle had been proved as an article of diet, that it was once a favourite vegetable in Scotland, that it had helped to keep people alive at the time of the Irish famine, and that even during the recent war it had been recommended as an excellent substitute for spinach. \"May we not put it in this way,\" he would ask, \"that you call a nettle useless merely because you yourself do not make use of it?\" \"It seems that you are right, O Socrates.\" \"And would you call an aeroplane useless, merely because you yourself have never made use of an aeroplane? Or a pig useless, merely because you yourself do not eat pork?\" There would be a great wagging of heads among the opponents, after which a third would pluck up courage to say: \"But, surely, Socrates, nettles as we know them to-day are simply noxious plants that fulfil no function but to sting our children?\" Socrates would say, after a moment's pause: \"That certainly is an argument that deserves serious consideration. A weed, then, is to be condemned, you think, not for its uselessness, but for its noxiousness?\" This would be agreed to. \"Then,\" he would pursue his questions, \"you would probably call monkshood a weed, seeing that it has been the cause not merely of pain but even of death itself to many children.\" His opponent would grow angry at this, and exclaim: \"Why, I cultivate monkshood in my own garden. It is one of the most beautiful of the flowers.\" Then there would be some wrangling as to whether ugliness was the test of weeds, till Socrates would make it clear that this would involve omitting speedwell and the scarlet pimpernel from the list. Someone else would contend that the essence of a weed was its troublesomeness, but Socrates would counter this by asking them whether horseradish was not a far more troublesome thing in a garden than foxgloves. \"Oh,\" one of the disputants would cry in desperation, \"let us simply say that a weed is any plant that is not wanted in the place where it is growing.\" \"You would call groundsel a weed in the garden of a man who does not keep a canary, but not a weed in the garden of a man who does?\" \"I would.\" Socrates would burst out laughing at this, and say: \"It seems to me that a weed is more difficult to define even than justice. I think we had better change the subject and talk about the immortality of the soul.\" The only part of the definition of a weed, indeed, that bears a moment's investigation is contained in the three words: \"colloq., a cigar.\"")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("字典上说，杂草是“任何无用的、麻烦的、有毒的或生长在不需要的地方的植物”。字典还补充道：“俗称，雪茄。”为了当前的目的，我们可以省略无害的口语，但定义的其余部分值得仔细研究。我想，苏格拉底本可以找到一些尖锐的问题向词典制作者提出。他可能从两种最常见的杂草开始：荨麻和蒲公英。当他的对手——苏格拉底的对手都与夏洛克·福尔摩斯的华生医生有着相同的心理素质——急切地承认荨麻是一种杂草后，他会立即对这个定义进行检验。 “故事是这样的，”他引用了克拉克·纳托尔夫人的令人钦佩的著作《生长中的野花》，“罗马士兵把最毒的荨麻带到英国，当他们因这个——对他们来说——极其恶劣的小岛的寒冷而麻木不仁时，他们就用荨麻来鞭打自己。可以肯定的是，”他从同一来源补充道，“医生曾经用荨麻来蜇人。”瘫痪的四肢再次恢复活力，也能治愈风湿病，”他会问，“难道荨麻不是杂草，或者你对杂草的定义是错误的吗？”他的对手肯定会回答：“确实如此，苏格拉底啊。”然而，第二个对手却会鲁莽地接受这场争论。他会指出，即使罗马人错误地认为荨麻蜇伤可以预防脚冷，并且如果我们迷信的祖先利用荨麻可以用来治疗风湿病，正如我们迷信的同时代人为了同样的目的而诉诸蜂蜇一样，荨麻在任何时候都可能毫无用处，而且在今天当然也毫无用处。苏格拉底会带着平静的微笑转向他，问道：“当我们说植物无用时，我们是否仅仅意味着我们实际上没有利用它，或者即使我们利用了它也没有什么用处？”答案会跳出来：“毫无疑问是后者，苏格拉底啊。”然后，苏格拉底会再次想起他的纳托尔夫人，并提到一种古老的草药，它声称每天服用一些荨麻籽可以“减少过度肥胖”。他承认他从未尝试过这种疗法，因为他不想摆脱诸神赋予他的肥胖。然而，他会声称，荨麻作为一种饮食的有用性已被证明，它曾经是苏格兰最受欢迎的蔬菜，它在爱尔兰饥荒时期帮助人们维持生命，甚至在最近的战争期间，它也被推荐为菠菜的绝佳替代品。 “难道我们不能这么说吗？”他会问，“你仅仅因为自己不使用荨麻就认为它毫无用处？” “看来你是对的，苏格拉底啊。” “你会仅仅因为你自己从未使用过飞机，就说飞机没用吗？或者仅仅因为你自己不吃猪肉，就说猪没用吗？”反对者们会纷纷摇头，之后第三个人会鼓起勇气说：“但是，当然，苏格拉底，我们今天所知道的荨麻只是有毒的植物，除了蜇我们的孩子之外没有任何功能？”苏格拉底停顿片刻后会说：“这当然是一个值得认真考虑的论点。那么，你认为杂草应该受到谴责，不是因为它无用，而是因为它有毒？”这会被同意。“然后，”他会继续他的问题，“你可能会称附子为杂草，因为它不仅导致许多孩子的痛苦，甚至导致死亡。”他的对手会对此生气，并惊呼：“为什么，我在自己的花园里种植附子。它是最美丽的花之一。”然后，就会有人争论是否丑陋是对杂草的考验，直到苏格拉底明确表示，这将涉及从名单中删除斯皮德威尔和猩红色的粉刺花。其他人会争辩说杂草的本质是它的麻烦，但苏格拉底会反驳这一点，问他们辣根在花园里是否不是比毛地黄更麻烦的东西。“哦，”其中之一争论者会绝望地喊道：“我们简单地说，杂草是指那些不养金丝雀的人的花园里的杂草，而不是养金丝雀的人的花园里的杂草？”我认为我们最好换个话题，谈谈灵魂的不朽。”事实上，杂草定义中唯一值得花点时间研究的部分包含在三个词中：“colloq.，一支雪茄。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("In my opinion, the safest course is to include among weeds all plants that grow wild. It is also important to get rid of the notion that weeds are necessarily evil things that should be exterminated like rats. I remember some years ago seeing an appalling suggestion that farmers should be compelled by law to clear their land of weeds. The writer, if I remember correctly, even looked forward to the day when a farmer would be fined if a daisy were found growing in one of his fields. Utilitarianism of this kind terrifies the imagination. There are some people who are aghast at the prospect of a world of simplified spelling. But a world of simplified spelling would be Arcadia itself compared to a world without wild flowers. According to certain writers in The Times, however, we are faced with the possibility of a world without wild flowers, even if the Board of Agriculture takes no hand in the business. These writers tell us that the reckless plucking of wild flowers has already led to a great diminution in their numbers. Daffodils grow wild in many parts of England, but, as soon as they appear, hordes of holiday-makers rush to the scene and gather them in such numbers as to injure the life of the plants. I am not enough of a botanist to know whether it is possible in this way to discourage flowers that grow from bulbs. If it is, it seems likely enough that, with the increasing popularity of country walks, there will after a time be no daffodils or orchises left in England. If one were sure of it, one would never pluck a bee-orchis again. One does not know why one plucks it, except that the bee-shaped flower is one of the most exquisite of Nature's toys, and one is greedy of possessing it. Children try to catch butterflies for the same reason. If it were possible to catch a sunset or a blue sea, no doubt we should take them home with us, too. It may be that art is only the transmuted instinct to seize and make our own all the beautiful things we see. The collector of birds' eggs and the painter are both collectors of a beauty that can be known only in hints and fragments. Still, the painter is justified by the fact that his borrowings actually add to the number of beautiful things. If the collector of eggs and the gatherer of flowers can be shown to be actually anti-social in their greed, we cannot be so enthusiastic about them. I confess that on these matters I have an open mind. For all I know, the discussion on wild flowers in The Times may be merely a scare. At the same time, it seems reasonable to believe that if flowers that propagate themselves from seed were all gathered as soon as they appeared, there would before long be no flowers left. I notice that one suggestion has been made to the effect that flower-lovers should provide themselves with seeds and should scatter these in \"likely places\" during their country walks. I do not like this plotting on Nature's behalf. Besides, it might lead to some rather difficult situations. If this general seed-sowing became a matter of principle, for instance, I should probably sow daisies on my neighbour's tennis lawn, poppies and fumitory in his cornfield, and dandelions in his meadow. It is not that I am devoted to the dandelion as a flower, though it has been praised for its beauty, but at a later stage a meadow of a million dandelion-clocks seems to me to be one of the most beautiful of spectacles. But I would go further than this. I should never see a hill-side cultivated without going out at night and sowing it with the seeds of gorse and thistle. Not that I should bear any ill-will to the farmer, but it is said that the diminution of waste land, with its abundance of gorse and thistles, has led to a great diminution in the number of linnets and goldfinches. The farmer, perhaps, can do without linnets and goldfinches, but we who make our living in other ways cannot. I should sow tares among his wheat, if necessary, if I believed that tares would tempt a bearded tit or a golden oriole.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("在我看来，最安全的做法是将所有野生植物都纳入杂草之中。同样重要的是要摆脱这样的观念：杂草一定是邪恶的东西，应该像老鼠一样被消灭。我记得几年前看到一个令人震惊的建议，即应该依法强制农民清除土地上的杂草。如果我没记错的话，作者甚至期待有一天，如果发现农民的田地里长着雏菊，他就会被罚款。这种功利主义令人恐惧。有些人对简化拼写的世界前景感到震惊。但与没有野花的世界相比，简化拼写的世界将是阿卡迪亚本身。然而，根据《泰晤士报》的某些作者的说法，即使农业委员会不介入这项业务，我们也可能面临一个没有野花的世界。这些作家告诉我们，对野花的不计后果的采摘已经导致它们的数量大幅减少。水仙花在英格兰的许多地方都有野生，但一旦它们出现，成群结队的度假者就会蜂拥而至，大量聚集水仙花，以致损害了植物的生命。我不是一个足够的植物学家，不知道是否可以通过这种方式阻止球茎生长的花朵。如果是这样，那么随着乡村散步的日益流行，英格兰很可能在一段时间内将不再有水仙花或兰花。如果人们确信这一点，人们就再也不会采摘蜂兰花了。人们不知道为什么要采摘它，只是蜜蜂形状的花朵是大自然最精美的玩具之一，人们渴望拥有它。出于同样的原因，孩子们试图捕捉蝴蝶。如果有可能看到日落或蔚蓝的大海，毫无疑问我们也应该把它们带回家。也许艺术只是一种转变的本能，我们要抓住并创造我们所看到的所有美丽的事物。鸟蛋的收集者和画家都是只能在暗示和碎片中认识的美的收集者。尽管如此，画家的借用实际上增加了美丽事物的数量，这一事实是合理的。如果收集鸡蛋和收集鲜花的人的贪婪实际上是反社会的，我们就不能对他们如此热情。我承认在这些问题上我持开放的态度。据我所知，《泰晤士报》关于野花的讨论可能只是一种恐慌。同时，似乎有理由相信，如果通过种子繁殖的花朵一出现就全部被采集，那么不久之后就会没有花朵留下了。我注意到有人提出了一项建议，大意是花卉爱好者应该为自己提供种子，并在乡村散步时将这些种子撒在“可能的地方”。我不喜欢这种代表自然的阴谋。此外，这可能会导致一些相当困难的情况。例如，如果这种普遍的播种成为一个原则问题，我可能应该在邻居的网球场上播种雏菊，在他的玉米地里播种罂粟和蒲公英，在他的草地上播种蒲公英。它不是我把蒲公英当作一朵花来热爱，尽管它因其美丽而受到称赞，但在后来的阶段，一片拥有一百万朵蒲公英钟的草地对我来说似乎是最美丽的奇观之一。但我会比这更进一步。如果我不晚上出去播种金雀花和蓟的种子，我就永远不会看到山坡上有耕种。并不是说我应该对农夫怀有任何恶意，但据说，随着金雀花和蓟大量生长的荒地的减少，导致了红雀和金翅雀的数量大幅减少。农民也许可以没有红雀和金翅雀，但我们这些以其他方式谋生的人却不能。如有必要，如果我相信稗子会引诱长须山雀或金黄莺，我应该在他的麦子中撒下稗子。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("Still, I cannot easily persuade myself that a Society for the Protection of Weeds is even now necessary. I have great faith in weeds. If they are given a fair chance, I should back them against any cultivated flower or vegetable I know. Anyone who has ever had a garden knows that, while it is necessary to work hard to keep the shepherd's purse and the chickweed and the dandelion and the wartwort and the hawkweed and the valerian from growing, one has to take no such pains in order to keep the lettuces and the potatoes from growing. For myself, I should, in the vulgar phrase, back the shepherd's purse against the lettuces every time. If the weeds in the garden fail to make us radiantly happy, it is not because they are weeds, but because they are the wrong weeds. Why not the ground-ivy instead of the shepherd's purse, that lank intruder that not only is a weed but looks like one? Why not bee-orchises for wartwort, and gentians for chickweed? I have no fault to find with the foxgloves under the apple-tree or with the ivy-leaved toad-flax that hangs with its elfin flowers from every cranny in the wall. But I protest against the dandelions and the superfluity of groundsel. I undertake that, if rest-harrow and scabious and corn-cockle invade the garden, I shall never use a hoe on them. More than this, if only the right weeds settled in the garden, I should grow no other flowers. But shepherd's purse! Compared with it, a cabbage is a posy for a bridesmaid, and sprouting broccoli a bouquet for a prima donna. After all, one ought to be allowed to choose the weeds for one's own garden. But then when one chooses them, one no longer calls them weeds. The periwinkle, the primrose and the mallow—we spare them with our tongue as with our hoe. This, perhaps, suggests the only definition of a weed that is possible. A weed is a plant we hoe up or, rather, that we try to hoe up. A flower or a vegetable is a plant that the hoe deliberately misses. But, in spite of the hoe, the weeds have it. They survive and multiply like a subject race…. Well, perhaps better a weed than a geranium.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("尽管如此，我仍然无法轻易说服自己相信杂草保护协会现在仍然是必要的。我对杂草有很大的信心。如果给他们一个公平的机会，我应该支持他们反对我所知道的任何栽培花卉或蔬菜。任何一个曾经拥有过花园的人都知道，虽然需要努力工作才能阻止荠菜、繁缕、蒲公英、疣草、山楂草和缬草生长，但人们不必为了阻止生菜和土豆生长而付出这样的努力。对于我自己来说，用俗话说，我每次都应该把荠菜放在生菜旁边。如果花园里的杂草不能让我们容光焕发地快乐，那不是因为它们是杂草，而是因为它们是错误的杂草。为什么不是地常春藤而不是荠菜，这种瘦长的入侵者不仅是一种杂草，而且看起来像杂草呢？为什么不用蜂兰花代替疣草，用龙胆代替繁缕呢？我对苹果树下的毛地黄或墙上每一个缝隙里挂着小精灵花朵的常春藤叶蟾蜍亚麻都没有任何挑剔。但我抗议蒲公英和多余的花粉。我保证，如果耙子、疥螨和玉米蚶侵入花园，我将永远不会用锄头对付它们。更重要的是，如果花园里只有合适的杂草，我就不应该种植其他花。但是荠菜！相比之下，卷心菜是伴娘的一束花，发芽的西兰花是首席女主角的花束。毕竟，应该允许人们选择适合自己的杂草花园。但当人们选择它们时，人们就不再称它们为杂草。长春花、报春花和锦葵——我们用舌头和锄头一样对待它们。这也许暗示了杂草的唯一可能的定义。杂草是我们锄地的植物，或者更确切地说，我们试图锄地的植物。一朵花或一棵蔬菜是锄头故意错过的植物。但是，尽管有锄头，杂草还是有它。他们像一个主题种族一样生存和繁殖……。好吧，也许杂草比天竺葵更好。")]
